  "name": "LightingLab",
  "description": "Every light kind the engine has, in one rig, placed in WORLD coordinates -- every entry sets followCamera false, so azimuth is measured from world -Z and the whole rig is a fixed constellation the camera can be moved around without moving a light. The fixture's focal point is (0, 1.5, 0) with radius 4, so a distance of 2.0 radii is 8 m and every light's range is 48 m: six times its distance, which is what LightRig::expand gives every non-directional light and the reason a rig light's reach and its shader falloff agree exactly. Three pairs are pairs on purpose. point-a and point-b are 15 degrees apart at the same distance, so their spheres of influence overlap and the froxels between them carry two lights instead of one -- that overlap is the only place in the fixture where cluster occupancy is not 1. rect-large and rect-small sit at the same azimuth and elevation and differ only in size (3.0 radii against 0.06), which is the one comparison that isolates emitter area from everything else; their intensities are equal as ratios, so the rig's own nits-per-area conversion makes them the same brightness at the subject and a different brightness everywhere else. And sun and ambient are both directional, so the fixture always has two lights that never enter the froxel grid at all -- the control that says a measurement of cluster assignment is measuring cluster assignment.",
